{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-209",
              "name": "Unauthenticated Django DEBUG pages disclose application internals",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated Django DEBUG pages disclose application internals"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated requests can trigger Django technical error pages that disclose restricted application configuration and source details. The pages expose framework/runtime versions, traceback and filesystem paths, route configuration, and backend connection metadata, materially aiding targeted attacks even though secret and password values are masked."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated requests can trigger Django technical error pages that disclose restricted application configuration and source details. The pages expose framework/runtime versions, traceback and filesystem paths, route configuration, and backend connection metadata, materially aiding targeted attacks even though secret and password values are masked.\n\nAn Internet attacker can enumerate internal URL routes and learn deployment paths, framework versions, database/SMTP identities, middleware, installed applications, and storage locations without authentication. This information lowers the cost of targeting additional vulnerabilities; no raw signing key or password was obtained during validation.\n\nSet Django `DEBUG = False` in every production deployment and enforce the setting through deployment configuration rather than a developer default. Configure a generic production error handler and ensure unhandled exceptions are logged only to access-controlled monitoring. Review all public routes for unauthenticated exceptions, add regression tests that malformed requests receive generic responses, and restrict administrative and management endpoints behind authentication and authorization. Rotate any credentials if future review finds them exposed in unmasked diagnostics.",
                "markdown": "Unauthenticated requests can trigger Django technical error pages that disclose restricted application configuration and source details. The pages expose framework/runtime versions, traceback and filesystem paths, route configuration, and backend connection metadata, materially aiding targeted attacks even though secret and password values are masked.\n\nAn Internet attacker can enumerate internal URL routes and learn deployment paths, framework versions, database/SMTP identities, middleware, installed applications, and storage locations without authentication. This information lowers the cost of targeting additional vulnerabilities; no raw signing key or password was obtained during validation.\n\nSet Django `DEBUG = False` in every production deployment and enforce the setting through deployment configuration rather than a developer default. Configure a generic production error handler and ensure unhandled exceptions are logged only to access-controlled monitoring. Review all public routes for unauthenticated exceptions, add regression tests that malformed requests receive generic responses, and restrict administrative and management endpoints behind authentication and authorization. Rotate any credentials if future review finds them exposed in unmasked diagnostics."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-209",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/209.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Unauthenticated disclosure of contributor PII and unpublished documents",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated disclosure of contributor PII and unpublished documents"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The public contributor JSON endpoint exposes personal and editorial-submission data without authentication. Its returned file paths also point to documents that can be downloaded anonymously, allowing any internet user to retrieve contributor PII and unpublished submissions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The public contributor JSON endpoint exposes personal and editorial-submission data without authentication. Its returned file paths also point to documents that can be downloaded anonymously, allowing any internet user to retrieve contributor PII and unpublished submissions.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can collect contributor names, phone numbers, email addresses, physical addresses, social handles, workflow status, editor information, and uploaded document references. The referenced documents are retrievable without a session and may contain unpublished editorial material and identifying information, creating privacy, harassment, and premature-disclosure risks.\n\nRequire authentication and an appropriate contributor/editor authorization check before returning contributor records. Return only fields necessary for the authorized workflow, and avoid exposing phone numbers, email addresses, postal addresses, social handles, editor metadata, and workflow state to unauthorized users. Store submitted documents outside a public web root and serve them through an authorization-enforcing download handler with non-guessable references. Review existing media objects and invalidate or relocate any documents that were made public by this route. Add regression tests covering anonymous, contributor, editor, and administrator access to both the JSON endpoint and document downloads.",
                "markdown": "The public contributor JSON endpoint exposes personal and editorial-submission data without authentication. Its returned file paths also point to documents that can be downloaded anonymously, allowing any internet user to retrieve contributor PII and unpublished submissions.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can collect contributor names, phone numbers, email addresses, physical addresses, social handles, workflow status, editor information, and uploaded document references. The referenced documents are retrievable without a session and may contain unpublished editorial material and identifying information, creating privacy, harassment, and premature-disclosure risks.\n\nRequire authentication and an appropriate contributor/editor authorization check before returning contributor records. Return only fields necessary for the authorized workflow, and avoid exposing phone numbers, email addresses, postal addresses, social handles, editor metadata, and workflow state to unauthorized users. Store submitted documents outside a public web root and serve them through an authorization-enforcing download handler with non-guessable references. Review existing media objects and invalidate or relocate any documents that were made public by this route. Add regression tests covering anonymous, contributor, editor, and administrator access to both the JSON endpoint and document downloads."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Unauthenticated IDOR exposes draft contributor archives",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated IDOR exposes draft contributor archives"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The contributor download endpoint returns private submission archives to anonymous callers without enforcing the draft-status authorization check used by its companion permission endpoint. An attacker can enumerate contributor IDs and retrieve unpublished DOCX submissions and uploaded photographs."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The contributor download endpoint returns private submission archives to anonymous callers without enforcing the draft-status authorization check used by its companion permission endpoint. An attacker can enumerate contributor IDs and retrieve unpublished DOCX submissions and uploaded photographs.\n\nUnauthenticated internet users can download unpublished contributor materials, including document contents and photos, for records they do not own. The demonstrated archives contain draft submissions for contributor IDs `35` and `36`; the endpoint also returns a valid archive for ID `33`. This exposes private editorial content and associated personal data before review or publication.\n\nRequire authentication and object-level authorization in the download handler before opening or returning any contributor files. Reuse the same draft/review/published state policy as `check-download`, and bind the record to an authorized editorial role or owner rather than trusting the numeric path parameter. Return `403` for drafts and unauthorized principals, use non-guessable download references only as defense in depth, and add regression tests covering anonymous, non-owner, and authorized access to draft and published records.",
                "markdown": "The contributor download endpoint returns private submission archives to anonymous callers without enforcing the draft-status authorization check used by its companion permission endpoint. An attacker can enumerate contributor IDs and retrieve unpublished DOCX submissions and uploaded photographs.\n\nUnauthenticated internet users can download unpublished contributor materials, including document contents and photos, for records they do not own. The demonstrated archives contain draft submissions for contributor IDs `35` and `36`; the endpoint also returns a valid archive for ID `33`. This exposes private editorial content and associated personal data before review or publication.\n\nRequire authentication and object-level authorization in the download handler before opening or returning any contributor files. Reuse the same draft/review/published state policy as `check-download`, and bind the record to an authorized editorial role or owner rather than trusting the numeric path parameter. Return `403` for drafts and unauthorized principals, use non-guessable download references only as defense in depth, and add regression tests covering anonymous, non-owner, and authorized access to draft and published records."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Stored XSS via unauthenticated SVG contributor upload",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stored XSS via unauthenticated SVG contributor upload"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated contributor submission accepts an SVG containing active script and serves it back inline as `image/svg+xml`. When a user opens the uploaded file, the script executes in the application origin, enabling arbitrary same-origin browser actions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated contributor submission accepts an SVG containing active script and serves it back inline as `image/svg+xml`. When a user opens the uploaded file, the script executes in the application origin, enabling arbitrary same-origin browser actions.\n\nA remote unauthenticated attacker can persist JavaScript on the application’s media path. A user who opens the uploaded SVG executes attacker-controlled code in the application origin; the demonstrated impact is arbitrary DOM modification, with access to same-origin browser capabilities such as authenticated requests and page content.\n\nReject SVG and other active-content formats for contributor uploads unless they are required. If SVG support is business-required, sanitize it with a strict allowlist that removes scripts, event attributes, external references, and active SVG elements before storage. Serve uploaded content from a separate origin, or force downloads with `Content-Disposition: attachment` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`; do not serve untrusted SVG inline under the application origin.",
                "markdown": "The unauthenticated contributor submission accepts an SVG containing active script and serves it back inline as `image/svg+xml`. When a user opens the uploaded file, the script executes in the application origin, enabling arbitrary same-origin browser actions.\n\nA remote unauthenticated attacker can persist JavaScript on the application’s media path. A user who opens the uploaded SVG executes attacker-controlled code in the application origin; the demonstrated impact is arbitrary DOM modification, with access to same-origin browser capabilities such as authenticated requests and page content.\n\nReject SVG and other active-content formats for contributor uploads unless they are required. If SVG support is business-required, sanitize it with a strict allowlist that removes scripts, event attributes, external references, and active SVG elements before storage. Serve uploaded content from a separate origin, or force downloads with `Content-Disposition: attachment` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`; do not serve untrusted SVG inline under the application origin."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
